{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SjXKpKWrYxA14A4ttBLSXi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260521T010111\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 3.0/6 — Borderline Findings\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-21T01:45:49.393561+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: The Likert Blindspot\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n11\t**Review mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date**: 2026-05-21\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Paper Summary\n17\t\n18\tThe paper introduces a counterfactual pairwise measurement procedure that reveals a \"Likert Blindspot\": standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges that position-controlled pairwise comparison readily identifies. The pipeline combines LLM-based counterfactual style rewriting, NLI-based semantic equivalence gating, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Across 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash) × 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), pairwise comparison detects formality bias in 7/9 cells (p<0.05), Likert ATE in 1/9, and binarized Likert in 0/9. The binarized-Likert control isolates measurement structure (forced comparison vs. independent rating) as the mechanism. Cross-axis replication on verbosity (p=0.024) and robustness checks (n=500 replication, alternative rewriter, human NLI validation) support the findings.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Reviewer Summaries\n23\t\n24\t### R1 (Methodology & Experiments) — Overall: 3.0/6\n25\t\n26\t**Focus**: Experimental design validity, statistical methodology, data integrity.\n27\t\n28\t**Key strengths identified**: Binarized-Likert diagnostic is well-designed and genuinely useful; systematic 3×3 grid coverage; honest component attribution; direction-aware NLI gate for information-changing axes; comprehensive robustness battery.\n29\t\n30\t**Key concerns**: (1) Suspiciously regular numerical patterns across the 3×3 grid — win rates decrease in perfect lockstep across judges within each benchmark, which is unlikely given n≈50 consistent pairs per cell; (2) Table 4 discrepancy — text claims original preference tested on \"all 9 cells\" but table shows only 3; (3) Verbosity and register tested on only 1/9 cells; (4) Consistency filter (discarding position-inconsistent pairs) introduces unexamined selection bias; (5) Likert scale specification entirely missing (number of points, anchors, prompt); (6) Post-hoc power analysis methodology unspecified; (7) Human NLI validation has only 5 negative-class instances — FP rate CI is extremely wide; (8) Zero figures in a 7-page paper.\n31\t\n32\t### R2 (Novelty & Related Work) — Overall: 3.5/6\n33\t\n34\t**Focus**: Novelty assessment, related work positioning, significance, framing.\n35\t\n36\t**Key strengths identified**: Refreshingly honest novelty positioning; binarized-Likert diagnostic is the most novel element; RATE critique is constructive and well-calibrated; psychometric lineage properly established; direction-aware NLI gate is a useful minor contribution.\n37\t\n38\t**Key concerns**: (1) Core finding is a known result (Thurstone 1927) in a new domain — novelty ceiling is real; (2) \"First systematic quantification\" claim (line 136) needs qualification — practitioners already adopted pairwise for LLM evaluation; (3) Related work gaps — FLASK and Stureborg cited in bibliography but not discussed, missing broader BWS literature; (4) \"Interventional\" language overpromised without formal causal apparatus (no DAG, no SUTVA); (5) Holm-Bonferroni corrected p-values never reported despite claiming to apply correction — how many of 7/9 cells survive?; (6) Register axis (p=0.068) weakens rather than strengthens the narrative; (7) Direction asymmetry noted but not explained.\n39\t\n40\t---\n41\t\n42\t## 3. Roundtable: Consensus & Disagreements\n43\t\n44\t### 3.1 Consensus Strengths\n45\t\n46\t1. **Binarized-Likert diagnostic is the paper's strongest contribution.** Both reviewers independently identified this as genuinely clever and novel. By applying the same sign test to dichotomized Likert scores, the paper cleanly separates measurement structure from test choice. The 0/9 result is clean and informative.\n47\t\n48\t2. **Honest intellectual attribution.** The paper explicitly acknowledges component provenance (lines 060–072) and positions the contribution as a novel diagnostic application, not a novel framework. Both reviewers praised this.\n49\t\n50\t3. **Systematic 3×3 grid for formality.** Testing all 9 judge×benchmark combinations rather than cherry-picking is methodologically commendable.\n51\t\n52\t4. **Constructive RATE positioning.** The critique of RATE is well-calibrated — acknowledging its contributions while identifying the Likert measurement as the specific limitation.\n53\t\n54\t5. **Comprehensive robustness checks.** Scaled replication, alternative rewriter, human NLI validation, and original-preference control collectively address the most obvious objections.\n55\t\n56\t### 3.2 Consensus Weaknesses\n57\t\n58\t1. **Verbosity cross-axis claim rests on a single cell (CRITICAL).** Both reviewers flagged that C2 (cross-axis generalization) lists verbosity as evidence, but the verbosity experiment is conducted only on GPT-4o×LLMBar. A single cell cannot support a generalization claim. At minimum, 3 judges on one benchmark are needed to match formality's coverage.\n59\t\n60\t2. **Missing figures (MAJOR).** Zero figures in a 7-page paper. A pipeline overview figure and a visualization of the Likert Blindspot (e.g., paired dot plot of p-values across 9 cells for all three methods) would substantially improve communication.\n61\t\n62\t3. **Table 4 / original preference inconsistency (MAJOR).** Text claims testing across \"all 9 judge×benchmark combinations\" but Table 4 shows only 3 cells (LLMBar only). This is a factual discrepancy that must be resolved.\n63\t\n64\t4. **Holm-Bonferroni correction claimed but never shown (MAJOR).** Section 4 states the correction is applied, but no corrected p-values are ever reported. With marginal cells at p=0.042 and p=0.032, it is plausible that the corrected count drops below 7/9. This omission undermines the headline claim.\n65\t\n66\t5. **Presentation errors.** Section 3 says \"four stages\" but describes five (3.1–3.5); Table 2 caption references bold formatting not visible; Soumik (2026) has placeholder arXiv ID; several bibliography entries lack full author lists.\n67\t\n68\t### 3.3 Remaining Disagreements\n69\t\n70\t| Issue | R1 Position | R2 Position | Resolution |\n71\t|-------|-------------|-------------|------------|\n72\t| **Data regularity** | Suspiciously smooth numbers across 3×3 grid; possible extrapolation | Not flagged | R1's concern is legitimate — the monotonic lockstep pattern (GPT-4o > Claude > Gemini, same ordering in every benchmark with near-constant decrements) warrants per-cell N_c disclosure. Flagged as a question for authors rather than an integrity accusation. |\n73\t| **Novelty ceiling** | Not a primary concern (scored method 4/6) | Primary concern (scored novelty 3/6) | Both valid perspectives. The novelty is incremental but the binarized-Likert diagnostic provides genuine new insight beyond \"pairwise > Likert.\" Consensus: novelty is the paper's weakest dimension. |\n74\t| **Consistency filter bias** | Flagged as unexamined selection bias | Not discussed | R1's point is methodologically important. If style-biased pairs are disproportionately consistent, the filter inflates observed win rates. Per-cell consistency rates should be reported. |\n75\t| **Likert scale specification** | Critical gap — scale, anchors, prompt needed | Not flagged | R1 is correct — the ordinal compression argument depends on the specific Likert instrument. This must be specified. |\n76\t\n77\t---\n78\t\n79\t## 4. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n80\t\n81\t| Planned Experiment | Covered? | Quality |\n82\t|---|---|---|\n83\t| exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise | ✅ Table 1 | Full coverage |\n84\t| exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality (3×3) | ✅ Table 2 | Full coverage |\n85\t| exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity (9 cells) | ✅ Table 3 | Full coverage — central result |\n86\t| exp-005: Original preference control | ⚠️ Table 4 | Only 3/9 cells shown despite claiming 9 |\n87\t| exp-006: Scaled replication n=500 | ✅ Table 5 | Full coverage |\n88\t| exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control | ✅ Table 6 | Full coverage |\n89\t| exp-013: Human NLI validation | ✅ Table 7 | Adequate but small negative class |\n90\t| exp-007b: Verbosity full pipeline | ⚠️ Table 8 | Only 1/9 cells — insufficient for C2 claim |\n91\t| exp-008: Register axis | ✅ Table 9 | Appropriately framed as exploratory |\n92\t\n93\t**Coverage**: 7/9 experiments covered. Two experiments have coverage gaps (original preference showing 3 not 9 cells; verbosity on single cell). No planned experiment is entirely missing.\n94\t\n95\t---\n96\t\n97\t## 5. Consolidated Scores\n98\t\n99\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n100\t|---|---|---|---|\n101\t| Methodological Soundness | 4/6 | — | 4/6 |\n102\t| Experimental Rigor | 3/6 | — | 3/6 |\n103\t| Statistical Analysis | 3/6 | — | 3/6 |\n104\t| Novelty | — | 3/6 | 3/6 |\n105\t| Related Work | — | 4/6 | 4/6 |\n106\t| Significance | — | 4/6 | 4/6 |\n107\t| Clarity | 4/6 | 5/6 | 4.5/6 |\n108\t| **Overall** | **3.0/6** | **3.5/6** | **3.0/6** |\n109\t\n110\t**Confidence**: 4/5 (both reviewers)\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## 6. Overall Assessment\n115\t\n116\t**Score: 3.0 / 6 — Borderline Findings**\n117\t\n118\tThe paper asks the right question and the binarized-Likert diagnostic is a genuinely clever contribution that provides mechanistic insight beyond simply observing pairwise > Likert. The 3×3 formality grid is systematic, the robustness battery is comprehensive, and the RATE positioning is constructive.\n119\t\n120\tHowever, several issues prevent a Main conference recommendation:\n121\t\n122\t1. **Novelty ceiling**: The core finding is a century-old psychometric principle applied to a new domain. The binarized-Likert diagnostic adds value but is a single control experiment, not a methodological advance.\n123\t\n124\t2. **Cross-axis claim is underpowered**: The contribution listing claims cross-axis generalization (C2), but verbosity evidence comes from a single judge×benchmark cell. This claim is not adequately supported.\n125\t\n126\t3. **Statistical reporting gaps**: Holm-Bonferroni corrected p-values are never shown despite being claimed. The headline \"7/9 significant\" may not survive correction. Likert scale specification is absent. Consistency filter bias is unexamined.\n127\t\n128\t4. **Data presentation concerns**: The suspiciously regular numerical patterns across the 3×3 grid deserve explanation. Per-cell sample sizes (N_c) should be reported for all 9 cells, not just LLMBar.\n129\t\n130\t5. **Table 4 factual discrepancy**: Claims all 9 cells tested but shows only 3.\n131\t\n132\t### Path to Acceptance\n133\t\n134\tTo reach Main conference quality (4.0), the paper would need:\n135\t- Report Holm-Bonferroni corrected p-values for Table 3\n136\t- Extend verbosity to at least 3 judges on 1 benchmark\n137\t- Resolve Table 4 discrepancy (show all 9 cells or correct the claim)\n138\t- Report per-cell N_c for all 9 cells in Table 3\n139\t- Specify the Likert instrument (scale, anchors, prompt)\n140\t- Add at least one figure (pipeline overview or Likert Blindspot visualization)\n141\t- Discuss consistency filter and potential selection bias\n142\t\n143\tAs submitted, this is a solid Findings-level paper with a clear, useful empirical contribution that is somewhat limited in novelty and has addressable experimental gaps.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t## 7. Key Questions for Authors (Prioritized)\n148\t\n149\t1. After Holm-Bonferroni correction, how many of the 7/9 pairwise-significant cells survive at α=0.05? Please report corrected p-values.\n150\t2. Can you provide per-cell N_c (consistent pairs) for all 9 cells in Table 3?\n151\t3. Were AlpacaEval and MT-Bench experiments run independently with fresh counterfactual rewrites?\n152\t4. What Likert scale (number of points, anchors, prompt template) was used?\n153\t5. What fraction of pairs had tied Likert scores and were excluded from binarized-Likert analysis?\n154\t6. Table 4 claims 9 cells but shows 3 — which is correct?\n155\t7. Do you have verbosity results for Claude or Gemini?\n156\t8. What is the consistency rate per cell, and could differential consistency inflate observed win rates?\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PKKQtMAmiZekkqbGBt9ihD","session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"355f5c89-8290-459b-b61e-24d12fb91a26","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:53:22.826Z"}
