{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Udm9pMSJ3bLLrZX6KASW7g","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient information to write the review. Let me synthesize what I've learned about the paper's actual experimental results versus what was planned, examining the methodology, statistics, and experimental design.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Review\n\n**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Evaluation\n\nThe paper proposes a 4-stage protocol (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, position-controlled pairwise comparison, Likert baseline) and applies it primarily to formality bias in GPT-4o. The core measurement insight -- that pairwise comparison detects bias Likert scoring misses -- is demonstrated on a single judge-benchmark combination (GPT-4o x LLMBar). The method includes several robustness checks (NLI threshold sensitivity, consistency filter bias, rewriter control, scaled replication).\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: Well-designed position control.** Dual-direction pairwise with consistency filtering (only counting pairs where both orderings agree) is methodologically sound and eliminates position bias as a confounder. The 43% discard rate from inconsistent pairs (exp-012) shows no systematic bias (all MWU p>0.12), validating that filtering is selection on noise, not on signal.\n\n**S2: The binarized Likert control (exp-010) is clever.** By applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores and showing it remains non-significant (60.6%, p=0.296 vs. pairwise 69.1%, p=0.006), the paper isolates the measurement structure as the source of the gap, not the choice of statistical test. This addresses the most dangerous confound for C2.\n\n**S3: Logistic decomposition (exp-014) with proper paired-data correction.** Using GEE with exchangeable correlation to handle the non-independence of paired observations (98 pairs x 2 trials) is statistically appropriate. Formality OR=2.21 (p=0.010) remaining significant after controlling original preference (OR=1.82, p=0.052) strengthens the causal interpretation.\n\n**S4: NLI threshold sensitivity analysis (exp-011).** Win rate stability at 72-74% across NLI thresholds 0.70-0.95 demonstrates the result is not an artifact of the 0.90 cutoff.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): Generalization failure undermines headline claims.** The paper's actual data shows only 1/9 judge-benchmark cells significant for formality bias. Claude-3.5-Sonnet (52%, p=0.42) and Gemini-2.0-Flash (49%, p=0.55) show null results on LLMBar. The bias does not replicate on AlpacaEval or MT-Bench even for GPT-4o. The \"Likert Blindspot\" cannot be demonstrated where no underlying bias exists -- making C2 effectively a single-cell observation. The title and abstract imply generality that the data does not support.\n\n**W2 (Major): Direction asymmetry and original preference confound.** The formal-to-casual direction shows 78.8% win rate (p=0.001) but casual-to-formal is 54.5% (p=0.832). This 24.3pp asymmetry is troubling. The paper estimates original preference at ~12.2%, but the GEE model only achieves marginal non-significance for original preference (OR=1.82, p=0.052). At n=500, the scaled replication shows the effect attenuates from 69.1% to 59% while original preference becomes significant (OR=1.55, p=0.025). The confound is not resolved -- it is confirmed at scale.\n\n**W3 (Major): NLI gate validation quality.** Cohen's kappa=0.43 is below the 0.60 threshold for \"good agreement.\" The paper's PABAK defense is valid (extreme base rate skew: 95 YES vs 5 NO), and precision=98.9% is reassuring. However: (a) the \"human annotator\" is Qwen3-32B itself -- the same model family that generated the counterfactuals, creating evaluator-producer bias (acknowledged in D021 but not adequately addressed); (b) FN rate=66.7% means the gate discards 2/3 of cases it flags, with only 4 true negatives to validate specificity; (c) with n=5 negative cases, any precision/specificity estimates have enormous uncertainty.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Multiple comparisons not corrected in reported results.** The paper plans Holm-Bonferroni correction but does not apply it to the current results. With 9 cells tested, GPT-4o x LLMBar's p=0.006 would survive correction, but the lack of systematic application is a methodological gap.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): Effect attenuation at scale raises concerns.** The n=500 replication shows the win rate dropping from 69.1% to 59%. While still significant (p=0.012), this 10pp regression is substantial. The n=100 result (from only 55 consistent pairs) had a wide CI [56.0%, 79.7%], and the n=500 estimate suggests the true effect is at the lower end. The paper's headline number (69.1%) is likely an overestimate.\n\n**W6 (Minor): Rewriter control is limited.** Only one alternative rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B) was tested, and it replicates the GPT-4o x LLMBar result (66%, p=0.022). This is necessary but not sufficient -- it doesn't address whether both rewriters share systematic artifacts (e.g., both trained on similar RLHF data favoring formal text).\n\n---\n\n### Statistical Rigor Assessment\n\n- **Appropriate tests**: Sign test for pairwise, GEE for clustered data -- correct choices.\n- **Power analysis**: Partially addressed. At n=55 consistent pairs, MDE is ~18pp above 50%, meaning effects below 68% are marginally detectable. The null results for Claude/Gemini could be genuine nulls or underpowered (58% would be undetectable).\n- **Multiple testing**: Acknowledged but not applied. Must be corrected.\n- **Effect size reporting**: Cohen's h reported for scaled replication; should be standard across all results.\n\n---\n\n### Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. Given the n=500 replication shows original preference becoming significant (OR=1.55, p=0.025), how do you distinguish \"GPT-4o prefers formal text\" from \"GPT-4o prefers original/unrewritten text that happens to be in the formal condition\"?\n\n2. With only 1/9 cells significant, what is the evidence that the \"Likert Blindspot\" is a general phenomenon rather than a property specific to GPT-4o's formality preference on short LLMBar texts?\n\n3. The NLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator -- the same model family as the rewriter. Have you tested with a truly independent annotator (human or different model family)?\n\n4. The casual-to-formal direction shows essentially null results (54.5%). Does this not suggest the effect is driven by degradation in the formal-to-casual rewriting direction rather than formality preference per se?\n\n5. At n=500, the binarized Likert approaches significance (p=0.063 per median dream estimates). Does this change the \"Blindspot\" framing to an \"efficiency gap\"?\n\n---\n\n### Does the Evidence Support the Claims?\n\n- **C1 (Method works)**: Partially supported. The protocol detects something in GPT-4o x LLMBar, but whether it is measuring formality preference vs. rewriting artifacts is not cleanly resolved.\n- **C2 (Likert Blindspot)**: Supported for one cell only. Cannot be tested where no bias exists. The binarized Likert control (exp-010) provides the strongest mechanistic evidence but remains a single-condition demonstration.\n- **C3 (Axis-dependent)**: Tone failure and verbosity/register NLI pass are useful method boundary characterizations, but scoring results are blocked -- this claim has incomplete evidence.\n- **C4 (Practical guidance)**: Insufficient -- no correction method validated.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3.5 / 6 (Borderline Conference / Findings)\n\nThe methodological design is careful with appropriate controls (position control, binarized Likert isolation, GEE decomposition). However, the generalization failure (1/9 cells), unresolved original preference confound at scale, and the evaluator-producer bias in NLI validation collectively weaken the empirical contribution. The paper would benefit from: (a) honest repositioning as a single-cell case study with methodological contribution, (b) independent human NLI validation, and (c) extended discussion of why the effect is GPT-4o-specific and what that means for the field."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aff9e06bb48cb2d11 (use SendMessage with to: 'aff9e06bb48cb2d11' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78121\ntool_uses: 15\nduration_ms: 104925</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"1ee7e240-18e3-4d61-be85-260bfee47d93","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:54:00.771Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), responsible for evaluating **methods and experiments** of an academic paper submitted to a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim**: Standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically underestimates style biases in LLM judges compared to pairwise comparison. Authors propose a counterfactual pairwise measurement protocol (style transfer + NLI verification + position-controlled pairwise comparison) and demonstrate this on formality bias.\n\n**Key findings**:\n- GPT-4o shows significant formality preference on LLMBar (pairwise win rate = 69.1%, p=0.006), which Likert scoring misses (p=0.332)\n- BUT: Claude-3.5-Sonnet (52%, p=0.42) and Gemini-2.0-Flash (49%, p=0.55) show NO formality preference\n- GPT-4o's bias doesn't generalize to AlpacaEval or MT-Bench\n- Only 1 of 9 judge×benchmark cells is significant\n- Scaled replication at n=500: effect attenuates from 69.1% to 59%, still significant (p=0.012)\n- Original preference confound: judges prefer original text over double-rewrites (OR=1.55, p=0.025 at n=500), more robust across judges than formality bias itself\n- NLI gate validation: κ=0.43 (moderate), high precision (98.9%) but poor specificity (33.3%), FN rate=66.7%\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama-3.1-70B replicates same pattern as Qwen3-32B\n\n**Method (4 stages)**:\n1. Counterfactual generation: rewrite text along style axis (formal↔casual) using instruction-tuned LLM\n2. Content preservation: NLI verification (bidirectional entailment ≥0.9)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise comparison: both orderings, only consistent pairs counted\n4. Likert baseline + binarized control for comparison\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — the full planned experiments):\nThe paper covers these experiments:\n- exp-001: Multi-judge formality pairwise (3 judges × LLMBar)\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark formality pairwise (3 judges × 3 benchmarks = 9 cells)\n- exp-003: Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity analysis across 9 cells\n- exp-005: RATE control (original vs double-rewrite preference)\n- exp-006: Scaled replication at n=500\n- exp-009: Non-Qwen rewriter control (Llama-3.1-70B)\n- exp-013: Human NLI validation (100 pairs, κ=0.43)\n\nThe review_context.yaml narrative says: \"Core generalization claim collapses. GPT-4o × LLMBar × formality is the sole surviving cell.\" The context predicts 1/9 significant cells, effect attenuation at scale, and that the original preference confound generalizes better than the formality bias itself.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate this paper's **methods and experimental design** in depth. Cover:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the 4-stage protocol well-designed? Are there methodological gaps?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is multiple testing handled correctly? Power analysis?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are the experiments comprehensive enough? What's missing?\n4. **Controls and confounds**: How well does the paper handle the original preference confound? The NLI gate quality?\n5. **Internal validity**: Does the evidence support the claims?\n6. **Reproducibility**: Could someone replicate this?\n\nProvide:\n- A detailed review with specific strengths and weaknesses\n- A preliminary score (1-6 scale)\n- Key questions for the authors\n- Assessment of whether the evidence supports the claims made\n\nKeep your review thorough but under 1500 words. Do NOT discuss novelty or related work positioning — that's R2's job.","agentId":"aff9e06bb48cb2d11","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient information to write the review. Let me synthesize what I've learned about the paper's actual experimental results versus what was planned, examining the methodology, statistics, and experimental design.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Review\n\n**Paper**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Evaluation\n\nThe paper proposes a 4-stage protocol (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, position-controlled pairwise comparison, Likert baseline) and applies it primarily to formality bias in GPT-4o. The core measurement insight -- that pairwise comparison detects bias Likert scoring misses -- is demonstrated on a single judge-benchmark combination (GPT-4o x LLMBar). The method includes several robustness checks (NLI threshold sensitivity, consistency filter bias, rewriter control, scaled replication).\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: Well-designed position control.** Dual-direction pairwise with consistency filtering (only counting pairs where both orderings agree) is methodologically sound and eliminates position bias as a confounder. The 43% discard rate from inconsistent pairs (exp-012) shows no systematic bias (all MWU p>0.12), validating that filtering is selection on noise, not on signal.\n\n**S2: The binarized Likert control (exp-010) is clever.** By applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores and showing it remains non-significant (60.6%, p=0.296 vs. pairwise 69.1%, p=0.006), the paper isolates the measurement structure as the source of the gap, not the choice of statistical test. This addresses the most dangerous confound for C2.\n\n**S3: Logistic decomposition (exp-014) with proper paired-data correction.** Using GEE with exchangeable correlation to handle the non-independence of paired observations (98 pairs x 2 trials) is statistically appropriate. Formality OR=2.21 (p=0.010) remaining significant after controlling original preference (OR=1.82, p=0.052) strengthens the causal interpretation.\n\n**S4: NLI threshold sensitivity analysis (exp-011).** Win rate stability at 72-74% across NLI thresholds 0.70-0.95 demonstrates the result is not an artifact of the 0.90 cutoff.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): Generalization failure undermines headline claims.** The paper's actual data shows only 1/9 judge-benchmark cells significant for formality bias. Claude-3.5-Sonnet (52%, p=0.42) and Gemini-2.0-Flash (49%, p=0.55) show null results on LLMBar. The bias does not replicate on AlpacaEval or MT-Bench even for GPT-4o. The \"Likert Blindspot\" cannot be demonstrated where no underlying bias exists -- making C2 effectively a single-cell observation. The title and abstract imply generality that the data does not support.\n\n**W2 (Major): Direction asymmetry and original preference confound.** The formal-to-casual direction shows 78.8% win rate (p=0.001) but casual-to-formal is 54.5% (p=0.832). This 24.3pp asymmetry is troubling. The paper estimates original preference at ~12.2%, but the GEE model only achieves marginal non-significance for original preference (OR=1.82, p=0.052). At n=500, the scaled replication shows the effect attenuates from 69.1% to 59% while original preference becomes significant (OR=1.55, p=0.025). The confound is not resolved -- it is confirmed at scale.\n\n**W3 (Major): NLI gate validation quality.** Cohen's kappa=0.43 is below the 0.60 threshold for \"good agreement.\" The paper's PABAK defense is valid (extreme base rate skew: 95 YES vs 5 NO), and precision=98.9% is reassuring. However: (a) the \"human annotator\" is Qwen3-32B itself -- the same model family that generated the counterfactuals, creating evaluator-producer bias (acknowledged in D021 but not adequately addressed); (b) FN rate=66.7% means the gate discards 2/3 of cases it flags, with only 4 true negatives to validate specificity; (c) with n=5 negative cases, any precision/specificity estimates have enormous uncertainty.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Multiple comparisons not corrected in reported results.** The paper plans Holm-Bonferroni correction but does not apply it to the current results. With 9 cells tested, GPT-4o x LLMBar's p=0.006 would survive correction, but the lack of systematic application is a methodological gap.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): Effect attenuation at scale raises concerns.** The n=500 replication shows the win rate dropping from 69.1% to 59%. While still significant (p=0.012), this 10pp regression is substantial. The n=100 result (from only 55 consistent pairs) had a wide CI [56.0%, 79.7%], and the n=500 estimate suggests the true effect is at the lower end. The paper's headline number (69.1%) is likely an overestimate.\n\n**W6 (Minor): Rewriter control is limited.** Only one alternative rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B) was tested, and it replicates the GPT-4o x LLMBar result (66%, p=0.022). This is necessary but not sufficient -- it doesn't address whether both rewriters share systematic artifacts (e.g., both trained on similar RLHF data favoring formal text).\n\n---\n\n### Statistical Rigor Assessment\n\n- **Appropriate tests**: Sign test for pairwise, GEE for clustered data -- correct choices.\n- **Power analysis**: Partially addressed. At n=55 consistent pairs, MDE is ~18pp above 50%, meaning effects below 68% are marginally detectable. The null results for Claude/Gemini could be genuine nulls or underpowered (58% would be undetectable).\n- **Multiple testing**: Acknowledged but not applied. Must be corrected.\n- **Effect size reporting**: Cohen's h reported for scaled replication; should be standard across all results.\n\n---\n\n### Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. Given the n=500 replication shows original preference becoming significant (OR=1.55, p=0.025), how do you distinguish \"GPT-4o prefers formal text\" from \"GPT-4o prefers original/unrewritten text that happens to be in the formal condition\"?\n\n2. With only 1/9 cells significant, what is the evidence that the \"Likert Blindspot\" is a general phenomenon rather than a property specific to GPT-4o's formality preference on short LLMBar texts?\n\n3. The NLI validation uses Qwen3-32B as annotator -- the same model family as the rewriter. Have you tested with a truly independent annotator (human or different model family)?\n\n4. The casual-to-formal direction shows essentially null results (54.5%). Does this not suggest the effect is driven by degradation in the formal-to-casual rewriting direction rather than formality preference per se?\n\n5. At n=500, the binarized Likert approaches significance (p=0.063 per median dream estimates). Does this change the \"Blindspot\" framing to an \"efficiency gap\"?\n\n---\n\n### Does the Evidence Support the Claims?\n\n- **C1 (Method works)**: Partially supported. The protocol detects something in GPT-4o x LLMBar, but whether it is measuring formality preference vs. rewriting artifacts is not cleanly resolved.\n- **C2 (Likert Blindspot)**: Supported for one cell only. Cannot be tested where no bias exists. The binarized Likert control (exp-010) provides the strongest mechanistic evidence but remains a single-condition demonstration.\n- **C3 (Axis-dependent)**: Tone failure and verbosity/register NLI pass are useful method boundary characterizations, but scoring results are blocked -- this claim has incomplete evidence.\n- **C4 (Practical guidance)**: Insufficient -- no correction method validated.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3.5 / 6 (Borderline Conference / Findings)\n\nThe methodological design is careful with appropriate controls (position control, binarized Likert isolation, GEE decomposition). However, the generalization failure (1/9 cells), unresolved original preference confound at scale, and the evaluator-producer bias in NLI validation collectively weaken the empirical contribution. The paper would benefit from: (a) honest repositioning as a single-cell case study with methodological contribution, (b) independent human NLI validation, and (c) extended discussion of why the effect is GPT-4o-specific and what that means for the field."}],"totalDurationMs":104925,"totalTokens":78121,"totalToolUseCount":15,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3894,"cache_read_input_tokens":72065,"output_tokens":2161,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3894},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2161,"cache_read_input_tokens":72065,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3894,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
