{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PKKQtMAmiZekkqbGBt9ihD","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the paper materials, experimental results, related work positioning, and prior dream review reports, here is my R2 review:\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work Positioning\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper combines counterfactual style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison into a diagnostic pipeline for detecting style bias in black-box LLM judges. The central claim — the \"Likert Blindspot\" — is that standard pointwise Likert scoring systematically fails to detect formality bias that pairwise comparison reveals (p=0.332 vs p=0.006 on GPT-4o x LLMBar). The authors position this as an application contribution assembling known components.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. The binarized Likert control is genuinely novel.** Applying the same sign test to dichotomized Likert scores isolates measurement structure (forced comparison vs. independent rating) from statistical test choice as the mechanism. I have not encountered this specific control design in prior Likert-vs-pairwise literature. This elevates the paper from \"pairwise beats Likert (known since 1927)\" to \"here is why, and here is a diagnostic that proves it.\"\n\n**S2. Honest contribution calibration builds trust.** The paper explicitly acknowledges component provenance (RATE for double-rewrite, Zheng et al. for position control, style transfer literature for rewriting). Calling C1 an \"application contribution\" and C3 \"preliminary evidence\" is appropriate. This avoids the trap of overclaiming a \"novel framework\" when the individual pieces are established.\n\n**S3. The RATE critique is well-positioned and constructive.** The observation that RATE's own Likert-based ATE may suffer from the very blindspot this paper identifies is an elegant reflexive argument. If RATE measures style effects via Likert ATE, and Likert ATE is insensitive to style effects, then RATE's null findings for style attributes may be false negatives. This is the paper's strongest positioning move.\n\n**S4. The tone negative control provides a principled method boundary.** Rather than claiming universal applicability, demonstrating that the pipeline fails for axes that change epistemic commitment (45% NLI pass, catastrophic failures) is more informative than a third positive result. This establishes where the method works and where it does not.\n\n**S5. Naming the phenomenon.** \"Likert Blindspot\" is memorable, citable, and accurately describes the finding. This follows the successful naming strategy of \"preference leakage\" (Li et al. 2026) and \"alignment tax\" (CASSE 2026).\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The core finding confirms a century-old psychometric principle — novelty is empirical quantification, not discovery.** Thurstone (1927) formalized why paired comparison yields finer discriminability than absolute scales. Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017) demonstrated this for NLG evaluation. Novikova et al. (2018) showed ranking detects quality differences that rating misses. The paper's contribution is demonstrating this in the specific context of LLM judge style bias — useful, but the framing occasionally slips into \"discovery\" language (\"we reveal,\" \"hidden bias\") when \"empirical confirmation of a predicted effect in a high-stakes domain\" would be more accurate. The psychometrics community would not be surprised by this finding; the NLP community might be, but that reflects disciplinary ignorance rather than genuine novelty.\n\n**W2 (Major). Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. (2025).** The related work section gives RATE one paragraph and Zhou et al. one sentence. For the two closest methodological predecessors, this is inadequate. A reader needs a comparison table or dedicated subsection articulating: (a) RATE targets general attributes via Likert ATE; CSD targets specific style axes via pairwise — and this paper shows why that matters; (b) Zhou et al. operate at persona granularity with Bradley-Terry aggregation; CSD operates at style-axis granularity with sign test + position control. These are genuine differentiators but currently implicit. The RATE differentiation paragraph in the related work is good but should be expanded to address whether CSD's NLI gate is strictly superior to RATE's post-hoc correction term, or whether they handle different failure modes.\n\n**W3 (Major). The \"Likert Blindspot\" title suggests generality that the evidence does not fully support.** The strongest evidence comes from 1/9 cells (GPT-4o x LLMBar). The paper claims 7/9 pairwise-significant cells in the full 3x3 grid, but: (a) Claude and Gemini show NO formality preference — meaning the \"blindspot\" is moot for those judges (there is nothing to detect); (b) the binarized Likert control, which is the mechanism isolation evidence, is shown for only 1/9 cells; (c) after Holm-Bonferroni correction for 9 comparisons, it is unclear how many cells survive. The title should either be qualified (\"...in GPT-4o\") or the binarized Likert control must be extended to all cells where pairwise is significant.\n\n**W4 (Major). 1/9 significant cells for the headline finding undermines practical significance.** If only GPT-4o on LLMBar shows significant formality bias, and Claude/Gemini are robust, then the practical implication is narrower than presented: \"GPT-4o has formality bias that Likert misses\" rather than \"LLM judges have style bias that Likert misses.\" The paper needs to either (a) demonstrate the blindspot across more judge-axis combinations, or (b) reframe the contribution as a diagnostic tool whose value is demonstrated on one important case.\n\n**W5 (Minor). Missing related work references.**\n- Stureborg et al. (2024) on inconsistent/biased LLM evaluators — directly relevant to measurement methodology\n- FLASK (Ye et al. 2024) — multi-dimensional evaluation with fine-grained scoring, relevant to per-axis assessment\n- Preference Leakage (Li et al. ICLR 2026) — validates the downstream impact chain (style bias propagates through RLHF), strengthening motivation\n- The broader Best-Worst Scaling literature beyond Kiritchenko and Mohammad\n\n**W6 (Minor). \"Interventional\" language still overclaims without formal apparatus.** The paper correctly avoids \"causal\" per the D020 language norms, but \"interventional measurement\" still implies a formal framework. Without a DAG, SUTVA discussion, or sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding, the paper provides \"manipulated comparison\" evidence — strong, but not formally interventional in the causal inference sense. The logistic regression (exp-014) is associational with measured confounders, not causal identification.\n\n**W7 (Minor). The correction component (C4) is thin relative to its positioning as a contribution.** Compared to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework (deployed in production, generalized linear model, full debiased leaderboard), C4 amounts to practitioner guidance bullets and a rudimentary win-rate adjustment. Listing this as a numbered contribution invites unfavorable comparison. It would be better positioned as a Discussion section implication rather than a formal contribution.\n\n---\n\n### Assessment: Does the Framing Match the Evidence?\n\nPartially. The paper correctly identifies the Likert Blindspot as its strongest contribution and de-emphasizes the method pipeline. However, the title and abstract suggest a general phenomenon (\"LLM Judges\" plural, \"Hidden Style Bias\" without qualification) while the evidence supports a narrower claim (GPT-4o has formality bias that Likert misses; Claude and Gemini do not have this bias at all). The framing would be better calibrated as: \"We demonstrate that the dominant evaluation paradigm can miss real style biases when they exist, and provide a simple diagnostic that practitioners should use alongside Likert scoring.\"\n\n---\n\n### Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. After Holm-Bonferroni correction across 9 comparisons, how many pairwise-significant cells survive at alpha=0.05?\n2. What does the binarized Likert control show for cells where pairwise effect is strongest (e.g., if Gemini x LLMBar at 72.4% — does binarized Likert reach significance there)?\n3. If Claude and Gemini show no formality bias under either measurement method, isn't the Likert Blindspot finding specific to GPT-4o rather than general to \"LLM judges\"?\n4. Would the blindspot persist with continuous scales (0-100 slider) or log-probability-based scoring? This determines whether the finding is about discrete Likert specifically or about independent rating vs. comparison more broadly.\n5. Can you provide the RATE ironic implication explicitly: if RATE measures style effects via Likert ATE, do RATE's own null findings for certain attributes potentially reflect this same blindspot?\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3.5 / 6 (Borderline Conference)\n\n**Justification:** The paper asks the right question, the binarized Likert diagnostic is genuinely novel, and the RATE positioning is well-executed. However, novelty is fundamentally empirical confirmation of a predicted psychometric effect rather than a new insight, the evidence base is narrower than the framing suggests (1/9 cells for the headline claim, 2/3 judges showing no bias to detect), and differentiation from the two closest predecessors (RATE, Zhou et al.) needs expansion. With revisions — extending binarized Likert to all significant cells, strengthening RATE/Zhou differentiation, qualifying the title/abstract scope, and reporting corrected p-values — this could reach 4.0. As described, it is a strong Findings paper that falls just short of Main on novelty grounds.\n\n**Confidence:** 4/5"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a06b4de050a0fe844 (use SendMessage with to: 'a06b4de050a0fe844' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 48127\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 124122</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"08378b41-7bd9-48ee-a815-d12f3a23d81a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:54:40.593Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), responsible for evaluating **novelty and related work positioning** of an academic paper submitted to a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison\"\n\n**Core claim**: Standard Likert pointwise scoring systematically underestimates style biases in LLM judges compared to pairwise comparison. Authors propose a counterfactual pairwise measurement protocol and demonstrate this on formality bias.\n\n**Key findings**:\n- GPT-4o shows significant formality preference on LLMBar (pairwise win rate=69.1%, p=0.006), which Likert scoring misses (p=0.332)\n- Claude-3.5-Sonnet and Gemini-2.0-Flash show NO formality preference\n- Only 1 of 9 judge×benchmark cells is significant\n- Scaled replication at n=500: effect attenuates to 59%, still significant\n- Original preference confound confirmed (OR=1.55, p=0.025)\n- NLI gate: κ=0.43, high precision but poor specificity\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. A counterfactual pairwise measurement protocol assembling known components (style transfer, NLI verification, position-controlled pairwise) into a diagnostic pipeline\n2. Evidence that Likert scoring misses detectable formality bias in GPT-4o — implications for RATE and other Likert-based assessments\n3. Boundary analysis showing when counterfactual style measurement succeeds and fails (by NLI pass rate and text length)\n4. Comparative judge robustness finding: Claude/Gemini formality-robust, GPT-4o is not\n\n**Related Work covered in paper**:\n- LLM Judge Biases: Zheng et al. 2023 (position/verbosity bias), SOS-Bench 2025 (style features explain R=0.999 variance), CASSE 2026 (12×12 style matrix), FairJudge 2025, Preference Leakage Li et al. 2026, Stureborg et al. 2024\n- Likert vs Pairwise Measurement: Thurstone 1927, Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017 (BWS outperforms Likert), Novikova et al. 2018, Karpinska et al. 2021\n- Counterfactual Text Methods: Veitch et al. 2020, RATE 2025 (double-rewrite ATE + Likert), Zhou et al. 2025 (persona-level counterfactuals + BT), Mir et al. 2019, Krishna et al. 2020\n- Length Bias Correction: LC-AlpacaEval Li et al. 2024\n\n**Method**: 4-stage pipeline — (1) counterfactual style transfer, (2) NLI content verification, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Likert baseline + binarized control.\n\n## Your Task\n\nEvaluate this paper's **novelty and related work** in depth. Cover:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the contribution? Is assembling known components into a pipeline sufficient novelty for a top venue? Is the \"Likert Blindspot\" a genuinely new insight or a known psychometric fact applied to a new domain?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there important missing references? Is the positioning fair to prior work?\n3. **Differentiation from RATE**: The paper directly extends RATE (2025) by substituting pairwise for Likert. Is this delta sufficient?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Given that the main finding (Likert Blindspot) only manifests in 1/9 cells, how significant is the contribution?\n5. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-framed given its results? Does the title oversell (\"The Likert Blindspot\" suggests a general phenomenon, but it's demonstrated only for GPT-4o × LLMBar × formality)?\n6. **Impact potential**: Who benefits from this work? Is the diagnostic protocol valuable even if the formality finding is narrow?\n\nProvide:\n- A detailed review with specific strengths and weaknesses\n- A preliminary score (1-6 scale)\n- Key questions for the authors\n- Assessment of whether the framing matches the evidence\n\nKeep your review thorough but under 1500 words. Do NOT discuss methods/experiments in detail — that's R1's job.","agentId":"a06b4de050a0fe844","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the paper materials, experimental results, related work positioning, and prior dream review reports, here is my R2 review:\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work Positioning\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper combines counterfactual style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison into a diagnostic pipeline for detecting style bias in black-box LLM judges. The central claim — the \"Likert Blindspot\" — is that standard pointwise Likert scoring systematically fails to detect formality bias that pairwise comparison reveals (p=0.332 vs p=0.006 on GPT-4o x LLMBar). The authors position this as an application contribution assembling known components.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. The binarized Likert control is genuinely novel.** Applying the same sign test to dichotomized Likert scores isolates measurement structure (forced comparison vs. independent rating) from statistical test choice as the mechanism. I have not encountered this specific control design in prior Likert-vs-pairwise literature. This elevates the paper from \"pairwise beats Likert (known since 1927)\" to \"here is why, and here is a diagnostic that proves it.\"\n\n**S2. Honest contribution calibration builds trust.** The paper explicitly acknowledges component provenance (RATE for double-rewrite, Zheng et al. for position control, style transfer literature for rewriting). Calling C1 an \"application contribution\" and C3 \"preliminary evidence\" is appropriate. This avoids the trap of overclaiming a \"novel framework\" when the individual pieces are established.\n\n**S3. The RATE critique is well-positioned and constructive.** The observation that RATE's own Likert-based ATE may suffer from the very blindspot this paper identifies is an elegant reflexive argument. If RATE measures style effects via Likert ATE, and Likert ATE is insensitive to style effects, then RATE's null findings for style attributes may be false negatives. This is the paper's strongest positioning move.\n\n**S4. The tone negative control provides a principled method boundary.** Rather than claiming universal applicability, demonstrating that the pipeline fails for axes that change epistemic commitment (45% NLI pass, catastrophic failures) is more informative than a third positive result. This establishes where the method works and where it does not.\n\n**S5. Naming the phenomenon.** \"Likert Blindspot\" is memorable, citable, and accurately describes the finding. This follows the successful naming strategy of \"preference leakage\" (Li et al. 2026) and \"alignment tax\" (CASSE 2026).\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The core finding confirms a century-old psychometric principle — novelty is empirical quantification, not discovery.** Thurstone (1927) formalized why paired comparison yields finer discriminability than absolute scales. Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017) demonstrated this for NLG evaluation. Novikova et al. (2018) showed ranking detects quality differences that rating misses. The paper's contribution is demonstrating this in the specific context of LLM judge style bias — useful, but the framing occasionally slips into \"discovery\" language (\"we reveal,\" \"hidden bias\") when \"empirical confirmation of a predicted effect in a high-stakes domain\" would be more accurate. The psychometrics community would not be surprised by this finding; the NLP community might be, but that reflects disciplinary ignorance rather than genuine novelty.\n\n**W2 (Major). Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. (2025).** The related work section gives RATE one paragraph and Zhou et al. one sentence. For the two closest methodological predecessors, this is inadequate. A reader needs a comparison table or dedicated subsection articulating: (a) RATE targets general attributes via Likert ATE; CSD targets specific style axes via pairwise — and this paper shows why that matters; (b) Zhou et al. operate at persona granularity with Bradley-Terry aggregation; CSD operates at style-axis granularity with sign test + position control. These are genuine differentiators but currently implicit. The RATE differentiation paragraph in the related work is good but should be expanded to address whether CSD's NLI gate is strictly superior to RATE's post-hoc correction term, or whether they handle different failure modes.\n\n**W3 (Major). The \"Likert Blindspot\" title suggests generality that the evidence does not fully support.** The strongest evidence comes from 1/9 cells (GPT-4o x LLMBar). The paper claims 7/9 pairwise-significant cells in the full 3x3 grid, but: (a) Claude and Gemini show NO formality preference — meaning the \"blindspot\" is moot for those judges (there is nothing to detect); (b) the binarized Likert control, which is the mechanism isolation evidence, is shown for only 1/9 cells; (c) after Holm-Bonferroni correction for 9 comparisons, it is unclear how many cells survive. The title should either be qualified (\"...in GPT-4o\") or the binarized Likert control must be extended to all cells where pairwise is significant.\n\n**W4 (Major). 1/9 significant cells for the headline finding undermines practical significance.** If only GPT-4o on LLMBar shows significant formality bias, and Claude/Gemini are robust, then the practical implication is narrower than presented: \"GPT-4o has formality bias that Likert misses\" rather than \"LLM judges have style bias that Likert misses.\" The paper needs to either (a) demonstrate the blindspot across more judge-axis combinations, or (b) reframe the contribution as a diagnostic tool whose value is demonstrated on one important case.\n\n**W5 (Minor). Missing related work references.**\n- Stureborg et al. (2024) on inconsistent/biased LLM evaluators — directly relevant to measurement methodology\n- FLASK (Ye et al. 2024) — multi-dimensional evaluation with fine-grained scoring, relevant to per-axis assessment\n- Preference Leakage (Li et al. ICLR 2026) — validates the downstream impact chain (style bias propagates through RLHF), strengthening motivation\n- The broader Best-Worst Scaling literature beyond Kiritchenko and Mohammad\n\n**W6 (Minor). \"Interventional\" language still overclaims without formal apparatus.** The paper correctly avoids \"causal\" per the D020 language norms, but \"interventional measurement\" still implies a formal framework. Without a DAG, SUTVA discussion, or sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding, the paper provides \"manipulated comparison\" evidence — strong, but not formally interventional in the causal inference sense. The logistic regression (exp-014) is associational with measured confounders, not causal identification.\n\n**W7 (Minor). The correction component (C4) is thin relative to its positioning as a contribution.** Compared to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework (deployed in production, generalized linear model, full debiased leaderboard), C4 amounts to practitioner guidance bullets and a rudimentary win-rate adjustment. Listing this as a numbered contribution invites unfavorable comparison. It would be better positioned as a Discussion section implication rather than a formal contribution.\n\n---\n\n### Assessment: Does the Framing Match the Evidence?\n\nPartially. The paper correctly identifies the Likert Blindspot as its strongest contribution and de-emphasizes the method pipeline. However, the title and abstract suggest a general phenomenon (\"LLM Judges\" plural, \"Hidden Style Bias\" without qualification) while the evidence supports a narrower claim (GPT-4o has formality bias that Likert misses; Claude and Gemini do not have this bias at all). The framing would be better calibrated as: \"We demonstrate that the dominant evaluation paradigm can miss real style biases when they exist, and provide a simple diagnostic that practitioners should use alongside Likert scoring.\"\n\n---\n\n### Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. After Holm-Bonferroni correction across 9 comparisons, how many pairwise-significant cells survive at alpha=0.05?\n2. What does the binarized Likert control show for cells where pairwise effect is strongest (e.g., if Gemini x LLMBar at 72.4% — does binarized Likert reach significance there)?\n3. If Claude and Gemini show no formality bias under either measurement method, isn't the Likert Blindspot finding specific to GPT-4o rather than general to \"LLM judges\"?\n4. Would the blindspot persist with continuous scales (0-100 slider) or log-probability-based scoring? This determines whether the finding is about discrete Likert specifically or about independent rating vs. comparison more broadly.\n5. Can you provide the RATE ironic implication explicitly: if RATE measures style effects via Likert ATE, do RATE's own null findings for certain attributes potentially reflect this same blindspot?\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3.5 / 6 (Borderline Conference)\n\n**Justification:** The paper asks the right question, the binarized Likert diagnostic is genuinely novel, and the RATE positioning is well-executed. However, novelty is fundamentally empirical confirmation of a predicted psychometric effect rather than a new insight, the evidence base is narrower than the framing suggests (1/9 cells for the headline claim, 2/3 judges showing no bias to detect), and differentiation from the two closest predecessors (RATE, Zhou et al.) needs expansion. With revisions — extending binarized Likert to all significant cells, strengthening RATE/Zhou differentiation, qualifying the title/abstract scope, and reporting corrected p-values — this could reach 4.0. As described, it is a strong Findings paper that falls just short of Main on novelty grounds.\n\n**Confidence:** 4/5"}],"totalDurationMs":124122,"totalTokens":48127,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_read_input_tokens":45295,"output_tokens":2329,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2329,"cache_read_input_tokens":45295,"cache_creation_input_tokens":500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":500,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VskwgZdBQPvAKDXJXicuT3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have both R1 and R2 reviews. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE (R1 and R2 have provided independent reviews)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — I need to share R1's review with R2 and vice versa, let them react\nStep 3: Convergence — Identify agreements and disagreements, resolve\nStep 4: Synthesize — Produce final consolidated report and submit\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-share. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to react. \n\nI can handle both exchanges simultaneously rather than sequentially.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7950,"cache_read_input_tokens":55860,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7950},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"90b8a63b-e853-4f76-aec8-c4250b9e457c"}
