R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1 (Major): Core finding confirms century-old psychometric principle (Thurstone 1927) — novelty is empirical quantification, not discovery\n- W2 (Major): Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. (2025)\n- W3 (Major): Title suggests generality that evidence doesn't support (1/9 cells)\n- W4 (Major): 1/9 significant cells undermines practical significance\n- W5 (Minor): Missing related work references (FLASK, broader BWS literature)\n- W6 (Minor): \"Interventional\" language overclaims without formal causal apparatus\n- W7 (Minor): C4 (correction component) is thin\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review in 300-500 words. Cover:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R2?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you see things differently?\n3. **New concerns R2 raised**: Did R2 identify issues you missed?\n4. **Would you adjust your score?** Based on R2's novelty assessment, would you revise your preliminary score up or down?\n5. **Most critical issue for the roundtable**: What is THE single most important issue the roundtable should resolve?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7950,"cache_read_input_tokens":55860,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7950},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"fb6e3e7f-8cb3-42eb-bd95-09cd83fc508b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa96ec6dded1ea653","tool_use_id":"toolu_01A6ntST7mxRv1WC9vgDuDaF","description":"R1 reacts to R2's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), who previously reviewed the methods and experiments of \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison.\" You are now in a cross-sharing phase where you read R2's review and provide reactions.\n\n## Your Original R1 Review (Summary of Key Points)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**Strengths you identified:**\n- S1: Well-designed position control with consistency filtering\n- S2: Binarized Likert control cleverly isolates measurement format effect\n- S3: GEE logistic decomposition appropriate for clustered paired data\n- S4: NLI threshold sensitivity analysis shows robustness to cutoff choice\n\n**Weaknesses you identified:**\n- W1 (Major): Generalization failure — 1/9 cells significant, title oversells\n- W2 (Major): Direction asymmetry (formal→casual 78.8% vs casual→formal 54.5%) and original preference confound confirmed at scale (OR=1.55, p=0.025)\n- W3 (Major): NLI gate validation weak (κ=0.43, FN=66.7%, and annotator is Qwen3-32B — same model family as rewriter)\n- W4 (Moderate): Multiple comparisons not corrected\n- W5 (Moderate): Effect attenuation at scale (69.1% → 59%)\n- W6 (Minor): Limited rewriter control\n\n---\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**R2's Strengths:**\n- S1: Binarized Likert control is genuinely novel — isolates measurement structure from statistical test choice\n- S2: Honest contribution calibration builds trust (acknowledges component provenance)\n- S3: RATE critique is well-positioned — RATE's Likert ATE may suffer from this same blindspot\n- S4: Tone negative control provides principled method boundary\n- S5: \"Likert Blindspot\" naming is memorable and accurate\n\n**R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1 (Major): Core finding confirms century-old psychometric principle (Thurstone 1927) — novelty is empirical quantification, not discovery\n- W2 (Major): Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. (2025)\n- W3 (Major): Title suggests generality that evidence doesn't support (1/9 cells)\n- W4 (Major): 1/9 significant cells undermines practical significance\n- W5 (Minor): Missing related work references (FLASK, broader BWS literature)\n- W6 (Minor): \"Interventional\" language overclaims without formal causal apparatus\n- W7 (Minor): C4 (correction component) is thin\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review in 300-500 words. Cover:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R2?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you see things differently?\n3. **New concerns R2 raised**: Did R2 identify issues you missed?\n4. **Would you adjust your score?** Based on R2's novelty assessment, would you revise your preliminary score up or down?\n5. **Most critical issue for the roundtable**: What is THE single most important issue the roundtable should resolve?","uuid":"b6358b54-a4b3-485f-930b-ccd8b38d8619","session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1), who previously reviewed the methods and experiments of \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison.\" You are now in a cross-sharing phase where you read R2's review and provide reactions.\n\n## Your Original R1 Review (Summary of Key Points)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**Strengths you identified:**\n- S1: Well-designed position control with consistency filtering\n- S2: Binarized Likert control cleverly isolates measurement format effect\n- S3: GEE logistic decomposition appropriate for clustered paired data\n- S4: NLI threshold sensitivity analysis shows robustness to cutoff choice\n\n**Weaknesses you identified:**\n- W1 (Major): Generalization failure — 1/9 cells significant, title oversells\n- W2 (Major): Direction asymmetry (formal→casual 78.8% vs casual→formal 54.5%) and original preference confound confirmed at scale (OR=1.55, p=0.025)\n- W3 (Major): NLI gate validation weak (κ=0.43, FN=66.7%, and annotator is Qwen3-32B — same model family as rewriter)\n- W4 (Moderate): Multiple comparisons not corrected\n- W5 (Moderate): Effect attenuation at scale (69.1% → 59%)\n- W6 (Minor): Limited rewriter control\n\n---\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**R2's Strengths:**\n- S1: Binarized Likert control is genuinely novel — isolates measurement structure from statistical test choice\n- S2: Honest contribution calibration builds trust (acknowledges component provenance)\n- S3: RATE critique is well-positioned — RATE's Likert ATE may suffer from this same blindspot\n- S4: Tone negative control provides principled method boundary\n- S5: \"Likert Blindspot\" naming is memorable and accurate\n\n**R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1 (Major): Core finding confirms century-old psychometric principle (Thurstone 1927) — novelty is empirical quantification, not discovery\n- W2 (Major): Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. (2025)\n- W3 (Major): Title suggests generality that evidence doesn't support (1/9 cells)\n- W4 (Major): 1/9 significant cells undermines practical significance\n- W5 (Minor): Missing related work references (FLASK, broader BWS literature)\n- W6 (Minor): \"Interventional\" language overclaims without formal causal apparatus\n- W7 (Minor): C4 (correction component) is thin\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review in 300-500 words. Cover:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R2?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you see things differently?\n3. **New concerns R2 raised**: Did R2 identify issues you missed?\n4. **Would you adjust your score?** Based on R2's novelty assessment, would you revise your preliminary score up or down?\n5. **Most critical issue for the roundtable**: What is THE single most important issue the roundtable should resolve?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A6ntST7mxRv1WC9vgDuDaF","session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"2bc8ea0e-2955-4c78-a1fe-b771f837a086","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:55:01.488Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VskwgZdBQPvAKDXJXicuT3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XDoVSF7XnEkTJwb91SCbTM","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 reacts to R1's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2), who previously reviewed the novelty and related work of \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison.\" You are now in a cross-sharing phase where you read R1's review and provide reactions.\n\n## Your Original R2 Review (Summary of Key Points)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**Strengths you identified:**\n- S1: Binarized Likert control is genuinely novel\n- S2: Honest contribution calibration\n- S3: RATE critique well-positioned\n- S4: Tone negative control provides method boundary\n- S5: Good naming (\"Likert Blindspot\")\n\n**Weaknesses you identified:**\n- W1 (Major): Core finding confirms century-old psychometric principle — novelty is quantification not discovery\n- W2 (Major): Insufficient differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al.\n- W3 (Major): Title suggests generality evidence doesn't support\n- W4 (Major): 1/9 significant cells undermines practical significance\n- W5-W7 (Minor): Missing references, \"interventional\" overclaiming, thin C4\n\n---\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Score: 3.5/6**\n\n**R1's Strengths:**\n- S1: Well-designed position control with consistency filtering — 43% discard rate shows no systematic bias (MWU p>0.12)\n- S2: Binarized Likert control isolates measurement structure (60.6% p=0.296 vs pairwise 69.1% p=0.006)\n- S3: GEE with exchangeable correlation properly handles paired data non-independence\n- S4: NLI threshold sensitivity (win rate stable 72-74% across 0.70-0.95)\n\n**R1's Weaknesses:**\n- W1 (Major): Generalization failure — 1/9 cells, title oversells\n- W2 (Major): Direction asymmetry is troubling — formal→casual 78.8% but casual→formal only 54.5%. Plus original preference confound confirmed at n=500\n- W3 (Major): NLI gate validation quality poor — κ=0.43, and critically, the \"human annotator\" is Qwen3-32B (same model family as rewriter) creating evaluator-producer bias. Only 4 true negatives for specificity estimate.\n- W4 (Moderate): Multiple comparisons not corrected (though GPT-4o×LLMBar p=0.006 would survive)\n- W5 (Moderate): Effect attenuation at scale (69.1% → 59%) — headline number likely overestimate\n- W6 (Minor): Limited rewriter control (only one alternative)\n\n**R1's Key Questions:**\n1. How distinguish formality preference from original-text preference?\n2. What evidence that Likert Blindspot is general rather than GPT-4o-specific?\n3. NLI validation used Qwen3-32B as annotator — same model family as rewriter\n4. Casual→formal null results suggest degradation drives the effect?\n5. At n=500, does binarized Likert approach significance?\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R1's review in 300-500 words. Cover:\n\n1. **Points of agreement**: Where do you agree with R1?\n2. **Points of disagreement**: Where do you see things differently?\n3. **New concerns R1 raised**: Did R1 identify methodological issues that change your novelty assessment?\n4. **Would you adjust your score?** Based on R1's experimental findings, would you revise your preliminary score up or down?\n5. **Most critical issue for the roundtable**: What is THE single most important issue the roundtable should resolve?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7950,"cache_read_input_tokens":55860,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7950},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb8e4d9c-e5c9-4ff7-827a-6c216d215011","uuid":"c9fa5e80-7053-4481-82f6-5dc6936af216"}
